    {
      "id": "why",
      "type": "note.text",
      "position": {
        "x": 60,
        "y": -180
      },
      "params": {
        "text": "### What this graph answers\n\nThe same kind of connectivity as the first example, drawn as a circuit instead of a table:\nnodes are cell types, links are the synapses between them, laid out so the flow reads\nleft to right.\n\nType-level on purpose. A neuron-level network of this region is thousands of nodes and reads as\na hairball; the type graph is the diagram people actually draw by hand.",
        "outline": true
      },
      "size": {
        "width": 700,
        "height": 170
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "step1",
      "type": "note.text",
      "position": {
        "x": 892,
        "y": 430
      },
      "params": {
        "text": "**Collapse pairs into types.** Grouping by both ends of each connection turns a table of\nneuron-to-neuron rows into a table of type-to-type rows — which is exactly the edge list the\nnetwork node expects.",
        "outline": true
      },
      "size": {
        "width": 540,
        "height": 120
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "step2",
      "type": "note.text",
      "position": {
        "x": 1724,
        "y": 430
      },
      "params": {
        "text": "**Topology plus a table.** The network node emits node and edge attribute tables alongside the\ngraph, and every encoding in the viewer — colour, node size, link width — is a column picker\nover those. Its filters are not decoration: they change what the node passes downstream, and\nthe caption says how much was cut.",
        "outline": true
      },
      "size": {
        "width": 560,
        "height": 145
      }
    }
